From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Elsbrock <simon@iodev.org>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:08:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 24/27] fix(wlan): release RX reorder holes from a timer after
 100 ms

The RX reorder queue only gave up a hole from qmPopOutDueToFallWithin(),
i.e. when the next frame of the same TID arrived. The miss timestamp was
one per TID, set at the first hole and not restarted when that hole
filled and another opened, and the limit was 1 s.

A TCP sender whose data sits behind a hole receives no ACKs and stops
sending, so the next arrival is its retransmit, 200 ms to seconds later.
Every hole therefore stalled the download for at least one RTO and up
to the 1 s limit plus the next RTO, and a premature skip could also hit
a fresh hole that inherited an old timestamp.

Track when each frame entered reordering (rEchoBaTime from the previous
patch) and give a hole up once any frame queued behind it has waited
rx_reorder_timeout_ms (default 100, the mac80211 value), on arrival as
before and from a cnmTimer while frames wait. The timer runs in the
driver main thread, like the RX path, re-arms itself for the oldest
waiting frame, and delivers released frames through the normal
wlanProcessQueuedSwRfb()/kalRxIndicatePkts() path.

Released holes log a rate-limited "ECHO_WLAN_RX_BA: timeout gave up"
line; the RXSTAT ba line gains tmrRel (frames released by the timer).
The limit is tunable at runtime:

  echo 50 > /sys/module/wlan_mt/parameters/rx_reorder_timeout_ms
